      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML Certificate of Conformity document.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Certificate number: R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03, revision 1\n- Project number: 12200373\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 30 November 2012 (from signature section \"NMi Certin, 30 November 2012\")\n- Scheme: The certificate covers R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006 — this is a \"B\" scheme certificate (OIML Certificate of Conformity). The schema says scheme (A|B). OIML-CS certificates are typically B for this. Actually, given the year 2012, it's under the old OIML certificate system, which corresponds to \"B\" (Certificate of Conformity). I'll use \"B\".\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n\n**Applicants:**\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd., No. 555 Minshen Road, Songjiang Industrial Zone, Shanghai 201612, China\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (schema examples: \"Gas meter\" etc. — water meter is R49). Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Type designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- Module designation: not applicable / null — the cert mentions \"electronic calculating/indicating device\" but not module per se. I'll leave null or \"Not applicable\". I'll omit or set null. Actually the water meter is equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device — that could be a component. But no separate module designation is given. I'll set null.\n- Description: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model \"WATERFLUX 3070\", class 1 and 2. ... Water meter intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n\n**Model family:**\nThis cert covers many model variants — meter sizes DN25 to DN300, with accuracy class 1 and class 2. Each DN size has two variants (different Q3/Q1 ratios). Let me carefully parse the tables.\n\nPage 3 table (Accuracy Class 1):\n- DN65: Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio Q3/Q1=250, DN=65\n- DN80 (colspan 2): two variants: Q1=0.625/0.64, Q2=1.00/1.02, Q3=100/160, Q4=125/200, ratio=160/250\n- DN100 (colspan 2): Q1=1.0/1.0, Q2=1.6/1.6, Q3=160/250, Q4=200/312.5, ratio=160/250\n- DN125 (colspan 2): Q1=1.56/1.60, Q2=2.50/2.56, Q3=250/400, Q4=312.5/500, ratio=160/250\n\nCommon class 1: max admissible pressure 16 bar, temp 0.1/50 °C, indicating range 99.999.999 m³, verification scale interval 0.0001 (first 3 columns) and 0.001 (last 4), orientation all positions, environmental class C, battery 3.6 V, software versions.\n\nWait, verification scale interval on page 3: colspan 3 → 0.0001, colspan 4 → 0.001. Table has 8 columns total (1 label + 7 data). So first 3 data columns (DN65, DN80-1, DN80-2) get 0.0001; last 4 (DN100-1, DN100-2, DN125-1, DN125-2) get 0.001.\n\nPage 4 (continuation of class 1):\n- DN150 (colspan 2): Q1=2.50/2.52, Q2=4.00/4.03, Q3=400/630, Q4=500/787.5, ratio=160/250\n- DN200: Q1=3.94, Q2=6.30, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio=160\n- DN250: Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio=160\n- DN300: Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio=160\n\nMax pressure: colspan 3 → 16 (DN150-1, DN150-2, DN200), colspan 2 → 10 (DN250, DN300).\nVerification scale interval: colspan 4 → 0.001 (DN150-1, DN150-2, DN200, DN250), then 0.01 (DN300).\n\nPage 5 (Accuracy Class 2):\n- DN25 (colspan 2): Q1=0.025/0.040, Q2=0.040/0.064, Q3=10/16, Q4=12.5/20, ratio 400/400\n- DN40 (colspan 2): Q1=0.0625/0.100, Q2=0.100/0.160, Q3=25/40, Q4=31.3/50, ratio 400\n- DN50 (colspan 2): Q1=0.100/0.1575, Q2=0.160/0.252, Q3=40/63, Q4=50/78.75, ratio 400\n- DN65 (colspan 2): Q1=0.1575/0.250, Q2=0.250/0.400, Q3=63/100, Q4=78.75/125, ratio 400\n\nWait, ratio row: colspan 2 each → 400 for all.\nVerification scale interval: colspan 2 → 0.00001 (DN25-1, DN25-2), colspan 6 → 0.0001 (rest).\n\nPage 6 (class 2 continued):\n- DN80 (colspan 2): Q1=0.25/0.40, Q2=0.40/0.64, Q3=100/160, Q4=125/200, ratio 400\n- DN100: Q1=0.40/0.625, Q2=0.64/1.00, Q3=160/250, Q4=200/312.5, ratio 400\n- DN125: Q1=0.625/1.00, Q2=1.00/1.60, Q3=250/400, Q4=312.5/500, ratio 400\n- DN150: Q1=1.00/1.575, Q2=1.60/2.52, Q3=400/630, Q4=500/787.5, ratio 400\n\nVerification scale interval: colspan 2 → 0.0001 (DN80-1, DN80-2), colspan 6 → 0.001 (rest).\nMax pressure 16 bar.\n\nPage 7 (class 2 continued):\n- DN200: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400\n- DN250: Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 400\n- DN300: Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 400\n\nMax pressure: DN200 → 16, DN250/DN300 → 10.\nVerification scale interval: colspan 2 → 0.001 (DN200, DN250), then 0.01 (DN300).\n\nSo the model variants: Let me enumerate. Each DN size appears in both class 1 and class 2 with different flow rates (class 2 has ratio 400, class 1 has ratio 160 or 250). Some DN sizes have two variants within a class (differing Q3).\n\nApproach: define models by accuracy class + DN + Q3. E.g., \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\", \"DN80 class 1 Q3=100\", \"DN80 class 1 Q3=160\", etc.\n\nLet me enumerate all:\n\n**Class 1 (pages 3-4):**\n1. DN65, Q3=100, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q4=125, ratio 250, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n2. DN80, Q3=100, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q4=125, ratio 160, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n3. DN80, Q3=160, Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, Q4=200, ratio 250, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n4. DN100, Q3=160, Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q4=200, ratio 160, VSI 0.001, p=16\n5. DN100, Q3=250, Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q4=312.5, ratio 250, VSI 0.001, p=16\n6. DN125, Q3=250, Q1=1.56, Q2=2.50, Q4=312.5, ratio 160, VSI 0.001, p=16\n7. DN125, Q3=400, Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56, Q4=500, ratio 250, VSI 0.001, p=16\n8. DN150, Q3=400, Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q4=500, ratio 160, VSI 0.001, p=16\n9. DN150, Q3=630, Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q4=787.5, ratio 250, VSI 0.001, p=16\n10. DN200, Q3=630, Q1=3.94, Q2=6.30, Q4=787.5, ratio 160, VSI 0.001, p=16\n11. DN250, Q3=1000, Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q4=1250, ratio 160, VSI 0.001, p=10\n12. DN300, Q3=1600, Q1=10, Q2=16, Q4=2000, ratio 160, VSI 0.01, p=10\n\n**Class 2 (pages 5-7):**\n13. DN25, Q3=10, Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q4=12.5, ratio 400, VSI 0.00001, p=16\n14. DN25, Q3=16, Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q4=20, ratio 400, VSI 0.00001, p=16\n15. DN40, Q3=25, Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.100, Q4=31.3, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n16. DN40, Q3=40, Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q4=50, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n17. DN50, Q3=40, Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q4=50, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n18. DN50, Q3=63, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q4=78.75, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n19. DN65, Q3=63, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.250, Q4=78.75, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n20. DN65, Q3=100, Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400, Q4=125, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n21. DN80, Q3=100, Q1=0.25, Q2=0.40, Q4=125, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n22. DN80, Q3=160, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q4=200, ratio 400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n23. DN100, Q3=160, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q4=200, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n24. DN100, Q3=250, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q4=312.5, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n25. DN125, Q3=250, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q4=312.5, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n26. DN125, Q3=400, Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q4=500, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n27. DN150, Q3=400, Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q4=500, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n28. DN150, Q3=630, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q4=787.5, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n29. DN200, Q3=630, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q4=787.5, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n30. DN250, Q3=1000, Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q4=1250, ratio 400, VSI 0.001, p=10\n31. DN300, Q3=1600, Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, Q4=2000, ratio 400, VSI 0.01, p=10\n\nThat's 31 models. That's a lot but it's accurate. Let me create model_ids like \"DN65_cl1_Q3=100\" or simpler \"DN65-250\" hmm. The distinguishing key: accuracy class + DN + ratio (Q3/Q1) since ratio distinguishes the two variants within a DN+class. Actually class 1: DN65 ratio 250; DN80 has 160 and 250; DN100 160, 250; DN125 160, 250; DN150 160, 250; DN200 160; DN250 160; DN300 160. Class 2: all ratio 400. So model_id could be \"DN65 class 1\" and \"DN80 class 1 (Q3/Q1=160)\", \"DN80 class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\".\n\nFor class 2, all have ratio 400, and each DN appears once per class-2 table (except DN25/40/50/65/80/100/125/150 have two columns each!). Wait let me re-check page 5: DN25 has colspan 2 → two variants with Q3=10 and Q3=16. DN40: Q3=25, 40. DN50: Q3=40, 63. DN65: Q3=63, 100. Page 6: DN80: Q3=100, 160. DN100: Q3=160, 250. DN125: 250, 400. DN150: 400, 630. Page 7: DN200: 630. DN250: 1000. DN300: 1600.\n\nSo yes, class 2 also has two variants for most DNs. Model ID: use DN + accuracy class + Q3. E.g., \"DN25_class2_Q3=16\".\n\nLet me use a consistent model_id format: \"DN65 | class 1 | Q3=100\" or \"DN65-cl1-Q3=100\". I'll go with something readable: \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\".\n\nNow for `matrix_tables[]`, I should preserve the 5 tables (pages 3-7). Each table: name (page), columns, rows.\n\nThe tables have header \"Meter size\" with DN labels and colspan 2s. I'll flatten into rows where each row is a variant. Columns: accuracy_class (implied per table), meter_size, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, nominal_diameter, max_admissible_pressure, min_max_admissible_temperature, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval, orientation, environmental_class, power_supply_type, battery_voltage, software_versions.\n\nActually the schema example shows:\n```json\n\"matrix_tables\": [\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Flow characteristics by size\",\n    \"columns\": [\"size\", \"qmax\", ...],\n    \"column_units\": [...],\n    \"rows\": [...]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nI'll do 5 tables, one per page:\n\n**Table 1 (page 3, class 1, DN65-DN125):**\nrows:\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN65\", \"q1\": 0.40, \"q2\": 0.64, \"q3\": 100, \"q4\": 125, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 65, \"verification_scale_interval_m3\": 0.0001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN80\", \"q1\": 0.625, \"q2\": 1.00, \"q3\": 100, \"q4\": 125, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 80, \"verification_scale_interval_m3\": 0.0001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN80\", \"q1\": 0.64, \"q2\": 1.02, \"q3\": 160, \"q4\": 200, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 80, \"verification_scale_interval_m3\": 0.0001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN100\", \"q1\": 1.0, \"q2\": 1.6, \"q3\": 160, \"q4\": 200, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 100, \"verification_scale_interval_m3\": 0.001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN100\", \"q1\": 1.0, \"q2\": 1.6, \"q3\": 250, \"q4\": 312.5, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 100, \"verification_scale_interval_m3\": 0.001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN125\", \"q1\": 1.56, \"q2\": 2.50, \"q3\": 250, \"q4\": 312.5, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 125, \"verification_scale_interval_m3\": 0.001}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"DN125\", \"q1\": 1.60, \"q2\": 2.56, \"q3\": 400, \"q4\": 500, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 250, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 125, \"verification_scale_interval_m3\": 0.001}\nCommon: accuracy_class 1, max pressure 16, temp 0.1/50, indicating range 99999999, orientation all, env class C, battery 3.6V, software.\n\nHmm, indicating range \"99.999.999\" — European thousands separators. Should be 99,999,999 = 99999999. I'll write 99999999.\n\n**Table 2 (page 4, class 1, DN150-DN300):**\n- DN150: q1=2.50, q2=4.00, q3=400, q4=500, ratio=160, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN150: q1=2.52, q2=4.03, q3=630, q4=787.5, ratio=250, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN200: q1=3.94, q2=6.30, q3=630, q4=787.5, ratio=160, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN250: q1=6.25, q2=10, q3=1000, q4=1250, ratio=160, VSI 0.001, p=10\n- DN300: q1=10, q2=16, q3=1600, q4=2000, ratio=160, VSI 0.01, p=10\n\n**Table 3 (page 5, class 2, DN25-DN65):**\n- DN25: q1=0.025, q2=0.040, q3=10, q4=12.5, ratio=400, VSI 0.00001, p=16\n- DN25: q1=0.040, q2=0.064, q3=16, q4=20, ratio=400, VSI 0.00001, p=16\n- DN40: q1=0.0625, q2=0.100, q3=25, q4=31.3, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n- DN40: q1=0.100, q2=0.160, q3=40, q4=50, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n- DN50: q1=0.100, q2=0.160, q3=40, q4=50, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n- DN50: q1=0.1575, q2=0.252, q3=63, q4=78.75, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n- DN65: q1=0.1575, q2=0.250, q3=63, q4=78.75, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n- DN65: q1=0.250, q2=0.400, q3=100, q4=125, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n\n**Table 4 (page 6, class 2, DN80-DN150):**\n- DN80: q1=0.25, q2=0.40, q3=100, q4=125, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n- DN80: q1=0.40, q2=0.64, q3=160, q4=200, ratio=400, VSI 0.0001, p=16\n- DN100: q1=0.40, q2=0.64, q3=160, q4=200, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN100: q1=0.625, q2=1.00, q3=250, q4=312.5, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN125: q1=0.625, q2=1.00, q3=250, q4=312.5, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN125: q1=1.00, q2=1.60, q3=400, q4=500, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN150: q1=1.00, q2=1.60, q3=400, q4=500, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN150: q1=1.575, q2=2.52, q3=630, q4=787.5, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n\n**Table 5 (page 7, class 2, DN200-DN300):**\n- DN200: q1=1.575, q2=2.52, q3=630, q4=787.5, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=16\n- DN250: q1=2.50, q2=4.00, q3=1000, q4=1250, ratio=400, VSI 0.001, p=10\n- DN300: q1=4.00, q2=6.40, q3=1600, q4=2000, ratio=400, VSI 0.01, p=10\n\nNow `characteristics.model_level[]`: I need attribute × model matrix. With 31 models this would be huge but it's the most important part. Attributes that vary: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, verification_scale_interval, max_admissible_pressure, accuracy_class.\n\nThat's 8 attributes × 31 models = 248 value entries. That's large but let me do it. Actually, maybe I can keep the model list complete in model_family.models and provide model_level for the key varying attributes: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, verification_scale_interval, max_admissible_pressure, accuracy_class.\n\nHmm, that's a lot of JSON. Let me think about the best balance. The rules say \"Matrix detection — MOST IMPORTANT.\" So I should populate model_level properly. I'll do it.\n\nModel IDs — I'll define them per table entry:\nClass 1:\n- \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\"\n- \"DN80-class1-Q3=100\"\n- \"DN80-class1-Q3=160\"\n- \"DN100-class1-Q3=160\"\n- \"DN100-class1-Q3=250\"\n- \"DN125-class1-Q3=250\"\n- \"DN125-class1-Q3=400\"\n- \"DN150-class1-Q3=400\"\n- \"DN150-class1-Q3=630\"\n- \"DN200-class1-Q3=630\"\n- \"DN250-class1-Q3=1000\"\n- \"DN300-class1-Q3=1600\"\n\nClass 2:\n- \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\"\n- \"DN25-class2-Q3=16\"\n- \"DN40-class2-Q3=25\"\n- \"DN40-class2-Q3=40\"\n- \"DN50-class2-Q3=40\"\n- \"DN50-class2-Q3=63\"\n- \"DN65-class2-Q3=63\"\n- \"DN65-class2-Q3=100\"\n- \"DN80-class2-Q3=100\"\n- \"DN80-class2-Q3=160\"\n- \"DN100-class2-Q3=160\"\n- \"DN100-class2-Q3=250\"\n- \"DN125-class2-Q3=250\"\n- \"DN125-class2-Q3=400\"\n- \"DN150-class2-Q3=400\"\n- \"DN150-class2-Q3=630\"\n- \"DN200-class2-Q3=630\"\n- \"DN250-class2-Q3=1000\"\n- \"DN300-class2-Q3=1600\"\n\nTotal 31 models.\n\nFor each model, also attributes: nominal_diameter (constant per DN), temperature range (0.1/50 all), indicating range (constant), orientation (constant), env class (constant), power supply (constant), software versions (constant). Those go into type_level since they don't vary across models.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n1. minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h)\n2. transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h)\n3. permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h)\n4. overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h)\n5. ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n6. verification_scale_interval (m³)\n7. maximum_admissible_pressure (bar)\n8. accuracy_class (dimensionless)\n\nLet me write out values:\n\n**q1 (minimum_flow_rate_q1):**\n- DN65-c1-Q3=100: 0.40\n- DN80-c1-Q3=100: 0.625\n- DN80-c1-Q3=160: 0.64\n- DN100-c1-Q3=160: 1.0\n- DN100-c1-Q3=250: 1.0\n- DN125-c1-Q3=250: 1.56\n- DN125-c1-Q3=400: 1.60\n- DN150-c1-Q3=400: 2.50\n- DN150-c1-Q3=630: 2.52\n- DN200-c1-Q3=630: 3.94\n- DN250-c1-Q3=1000: 6.25\n- DN300-c1-Q3=1600: 10\n- DN25-c2-Q3=10: 0.025\n- DN25-c2-Q3=16: 0.040\n- DN40-c2-Q3=25: 0.0625\n- DN40-c2-Q3=40: 0.100\n- DN50-c2-Q3=40: 0.100\n- DN50-c2-Q3=63: 0.1575\n- DN65-c2-Q3=63: 0.1575\n- DN65-c2-Q3=100: 0.250\n- DN80-c2-Q3=100: 0.25\n- DN80-c2-Q3=160: 0.40\n- DN100-c2-Q3=160: 0.40\n- DN100-c2-Q3=250: 0.625\n- DN125-c2-Q3=250: 0.625\n- DN125-c2-Q3=400: 1.00\n- DN150-c2-Q3=400: 1.00\n- DN150-c2-Q3=630: 1.575\n- DN200-c2-Q3=630: 1.575\n- DN250-c2-Q3=1000: 2.50\n- DN300-c2-Q3=1600: 4.00\n\n**q2 (transitional_flow_rate_q2):** q2 = q1 × 1.6 typically. Check: 0.40→0.64 ✓, 0.625→1.00 ✓, 0.64→1.02? 0.64×1.6=1.024≈1.02 ✓ (rounded). Class 2: 0.025→0.040 ✓, 0.0625→0.100 ✓, 0.0625×1.6=0.1 yes. 0.1575×1.6=0.252 ✓. 1.575×1.6=2.52 ✓. 2.50×1.6=4.00 ✓. 4.00×1.6=6.40 ✓.\nValues:\n- DN65-c1-100: 0.64\n- DN80-c1-100: 1.00\n- DN80-c1-160: 1.02\n- DN100-c1-160: 1.6\n- DN100-c1-250: 1.6\n- DN125-c1-250: 2.50\n- DN125-c1-400: 2.56\n- DN150-c1-400: 4.00\n- DN150-c1-630: 4.03\n- DN200-c1-630: 6.30\n- DN250-c1: 10\n- DN300-c1: 16\n- DN25-c2-10: 0.040\n- DN25-c2-16: 0.064\n- DN40-c2-25: 0.100\n- DN40-c2-40: 0.160\n- DN50-c2-40: 0.160\n- DN50-c2-63: 0.252\n- DN65-c2-63: 0.250\n- DN65-c2-100: 0.400\n- DN80-c2-100: 0.40\n- DN80-c2-160: 0.64\n- DN100-c2-160: 0.64\n- DN100-c2-250: 1.00\n- DN125-c2-250: 1.00\n- DN125-c2-400: 1.60\n- DN150-c2-400: 1.60\n- DN150-c2-630: 2.52\n- DN200-c2-630: 2.52\n- DN250-c2: 4.00\n- DN300-c2: 6.40\n\n**q3 (permanent_flow_rate_q3):** matches Q3 in model id:\n100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 400, 630, 630, 1000, 1600, 10, 16, 25, 40, 40, 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 400, 630, 630, 1000, 1600.\n\n**q4 (overload_flow_rate_q4):** q4 = q3 × 1.25:\n- DN65-c1-100: 125\n- DN80-c1-100: 125\n- DN80-c1-160: 200\n- DN100-c1-160: 200\n- DN100-c1-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c1-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c1-400: 500\n- DN150-c1-400: 500\n- DN150-c1-630: 787.5\n- DN200-c1-630: 787.5\n- DN250-c1: 1250\n- DN300-c1: 2000\n- DN25-c2-10: 12.5\n- DN25-c2-16: 20\n- DN40-c2-25: 31.3\n- DN40-c2-40: 50\n- DN50-c2-40: 50\n- DN50-c2-63: 78.75\n- DN65-c2-63: 78.75\n- DN65-c2-100: 125\n- DN80-c2-100: 125\n- DN80-c2-160: 200\n- DN100-c2-160: 200\n- DN100-c2-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c2-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c2-400: 500\n- DN150-c2-400: 500\n- DN150-c2-630: 787.5\n- DN200-c2-630: 787.5\n- DN250-c2: 1250\n- DN300-c2: 2000\n\n**ratio_q3_q1:**\nClass 1: DN65: 250; DN80-100: 160; DN80-160: 250; DN100-160: 160; DN100-250: 250; DN125-250: 160; DN125-400: 250; DN150-400: 160; DN150-630: 250; DN200: 160; DN250: 160; DN300: 160.\nClass 2: all 400.\n\n**verification_scale_interval (m³):**\nClass 1: DN65, DN80-100, DN80-160: 0.0001; DN100-160, DN100-250, DN125-250, DN125-400: 0.001; DN150-400, DN150-630, DN200, DN250: 0.001; DN300: 0.01.\nWait, page 3 VSI: colspan3 → 0.0001 covers DN65, DN80×2. colspan4 → 0.001 covers DN100×2, DN125×2. Page 4: colspan4 → 0.001 covers DN150×2, DN200, DN250; then 0.01 covers DN300.\nClass 2: page 5: colspan2 → 0.00001 covers DN25×2; colspan6 → 0.0001 covers DN40×2, DN50×2, DN65×2. Page 6: colspan2 → 0.0001 covers DN80×2; colspan6 → 0.001 covers DN100×2, DN125×2, DN150×2. Page 7: colspan2 → 0.001 covers DN200, DN250; 0.01 covers DN300.\n\n**maximum_admissible_pressure (bar):**\nClass 1: DN65-DN200: 16; DN250, DN300: 10.\nClass 2: DN25-DN200: 16; DN250, DN300: 10.\nCheck page 4: \"Maximum admissible pressure (bar) colspan3=16\" covers DN150×2, DN200; colspan2=10 covers DN250, DN300. Yes.\nPage 7: DN200: 16; DN250+DN300: 10. Yes.\n\n**accuracy_class:** class 1 models → 1; class 2 models → 2.\n\n**nominal_diameter (mm):** could add as model_level but it's derivable from DN. I'll include it — actually it's constant per DN model. Include in model_level? It varies across models (25-300). I'll add it as model_level too: values DN: 65,80,80,100,100,125,125,150,150,200,250,300, 25,25,40,40,50,50,65,65,80,80,100,100,125,125,150,150,200,250,300.\n\nType-level characteristics (constant across all models):\n- minimum_admissible_temperature: 0.1 °C (hmm, \"0,1/50\" — min 0.1, max 50). Actually R49 temp classes: 0,1/50 means 0.1°C to 50°C (hot water meter T50? cold potable water typically 0.1/30, hot 0.1/50). So min/max admissible temperature: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure is model-varying (16/10) so model_level.\n- indicating_range: 99999999 m³\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\" — value \"Battery\", battery_voltage 3.6 V\n- software_versions: list [\"4.0.4_\", \"4.0.10_\", \"4.0.11_\", \"4.0.12_\", \"4.2.2_\", \"4.2.4_\"] — as a string or array? I'll keep as a string list... Schema says value can be number | {min,max} | string. I could do value as a string \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; ...\" or an array. Hmm, rule says value must be number|range|string per the structured value shape. But lists... For software versions, I'll use an array — it's a reasonable deviation. Actually to stay strict, maybe value: [\"4.0.4_\", \"4.0.10_\", ...]. The shape says number | {min,max} | string but an array of version strings is more informative. I'll use an array; it's still JSON-parseable.\n\n- accuracy_class overall: classes 1 and 2 → in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"] or [1,2]. Schema says accuracy_classes[] — I'll use [1, 2] (numbers) or strings? For R49 water meters, classes are \"1\" and \"2\". I'll use strings \"1\" and \"2\"? Hmm, values in characteristics must be numbers. For recommendation accuracy_classes I'll use [1, 2] as numbers... Actually water meter accuracy classes in OIML R49 are denoted 1 and 2 (numeric). I'll use [\"1\",\"2\"]... Let me just use [1, 2].\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical). The certificate references R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006 parts. Canonical: \"R49\". Edition: 2006. Amendment: null. Scheme: \"B\". Accuracy classes: [1, 2].\n\nHmm, the recommendation field has a single id/edition. Since parts 1-3 are referenced, maybe id \"R49\" with edition 2006, and maybe mention parts... I'll add nothing extra, or could use id \"R49\" and note parts elsewhere. Keep it simple: id \"R49\", edition 2006, amendment null, scheme \"B\", accuracy_classes [1, 2].\n\nActually scheme: OIML-CS — under the \"OIML Certificate System for measuring instruments\" it's scheme B (\"OIML Certificate of Conformity\"). Scheme A is MAA. Since this is a regular OIML Certificate of Conformity (NL1 issuing authority), scheme is \"B\".\n\n**Test reports:**\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, 41 pages + 14 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, 40 pages + 3 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, 40 pages + 4 annexes\n- R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01 (note typo \"1.2006\" — it's presumably R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01), 40 pages + 3 annexes\n\nRole: type_evaluation_report. Pages: 41, 40, 40, 40. Date: not given. I'll include pages and role. Maybe annexes count... schema fields: id, date, pages, role. I'll set pages as given numbers. For \"41 pages and 14 annexes\" → pages: 41. Could add annexes in... not in schema; omit or add? \"Don't fabricate\" — I'll keep to schema fields: id, pages, role. Dates unknown — omit.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- revision \"Initial\", date 29 November 2012, changes \"-\"\n- revision \"1\", date 30 November 2012, changes \"Adjustment in page numbering.\"\n\nThe schema says revision string e.g. \"0\". \"Initial\" → I'll use \"Initial\" or \"0\"? The document says \"Initial\". I'll record \"Initial\" — but schema example says \"0\". Rule: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). \"Initial\" is what's in the doc; I'll keep \"Initial\".\n\n**Footnotes:** None present. Omit.\n\n**Components:** The water meter is \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\" — that's a component. Role: \"calculating/indicating device\", type_designations: none given. Hmm, \"Don't fabricate\". There's no type designation for it. I could include a component with role \"electronic_calculating_indicating_device\", type_designations: []. Marginal — I'll include it since it's a named sub-assembly, with empty type_designations. Actually, maybe safer to include it in description only. The rule says components: \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\". The cert names \"an electronic calculating/indicating device\" as part of the meter. I'll include it with role \"electronic calculating/indicating device\" and characteristics noting it measures forward and reverse flow? Hmm. I'll include a minimal component entry.\n\nActually, I'll skip components — the calculating/indicating device is integral to the WATERFLUX 3070 (battery-powered electromagnetic meter, single unit), not a separately certified module. The description covers it. Hmm, but rule 6 says for complex instruments record components. This meter is presented as one type \"WATERFLUX 3070\" with no separate module designations. I'll include one component entry to be safe:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"electronic_calculating_indicating_device\",\n  \"type_designations\": [],\n  \"alternatives\": null,\n  \"characteristics\": {}\n}\n```\n\nHmm, that's pretty empty. I think including it is fine and harmless. Let me include it with a characteristic maybe: measures forward and reverse flow — no, that's a meter-level feature. I'll include the component minimally.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — the schema example categories include \"Water meter\" implicitly via \"Gas meter\" etc. Category: \"Water meter\".\n\n**model_designation**: The schema field is `module_designation` under certified_type: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. This meter — no module designation mentioned. Set null (omit) or \"Not applicable\"? I'll omit.\n\n**Certificate number field:** \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03 revision 1\". Number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\", revision 1 captured in certificate? The schema has revision_history. I'll put number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\" and maybe include revision in the number string? I'll keep number as \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\" and note revision in revision_history. Hmm, maybe add a \"revision\" field? Not in schema — but the schema says don't fabricate but adding informative fields... I'll stick to schema. Actually I could make number \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03 revision 1\" to be fully faithful. Hmm. The certificate number as printed includes \"revision 1\". I'll use number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\" and add revision_history entries which capture revision 1. That seems cleaner. Actually, to preserve full fidelity, I might include both... The layered schema certificate fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll set number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\", and revision_history captures it. Good.\n\noiml_issuer_id: NL1 (mentioned \"The Issuing Authority NL1\"). So certificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\", issuing_authority.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\ndate_issued: 30 November 2012 (the revision-1 date / signature date). Format: \"2012-11-30\" or \"30 November 2012\"? I'll use ISO \"2012-11-30\". Revision history dates: \"2012-11-29\" and \"2012-11-30\".\n\n**Characteristics type_level:**\n- temperature range: min/max admissible temperature {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C → attribute \"admissible_temperature_range\" with value {min:0.1, max:50}, unit_symbol \"°C\".\n- indicating_range: 99999999 m³\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\"\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V (the \"U_battery\" label: \"Battery 3,6 V\")\n- software_versions: [\"4.04_\", ...]\n\nLet me double check temperature: \"Min/max admissible temperature (°C)\" = \"0,1/50\" → min 0.1, max 50. Yes.\n\nIndicating range 99.999.999 → 99999999 m³.\n\nNow let me assemble the JSON. It'll be long due to 31 models × 8-9 model_level attributes. Let me be systematic.\n\nmodel_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"WATERFLUX 3070\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\", \"label\": \"DN65, accuracy class 1, Q3=100 m³/h\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": 65}},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, adding attributes per model in model_family would duplicate model_level. The schema shows models[] with model_id, label, attributes{}. I'll include nominal_diameter and accuracy_class and q3 in attributes to make each model self-describing, plus full model_level matrices. That's redundant but per schema shape. Actually maybe keep attributes minimal: {\"nominal_diameter_mm\": 65, \"accuracy_class\": 1}. Then model_level has the full matrix.\n\nLet me define the model list in order:\n\nClass 1 (12 models):\n1. DN65-class1-Q3=100\n2. DN80-class1-Q3=100\n3. DN80-class1-Q3=160\n4. DN100-class1-Q3=160\n5. DN100-class1-Q3=250\n6. DN125-class1-Q3=250\n7. DN125-class1-Q3=400\n8. DN150-class1-Q3=400\n9. DN150-class1-Q3=630\n10. DN200-class1-Q3=630\n11. DN250-class1-Q3=1000\n12. DN300-class1-Q3=1600\n\nClass 2 (19 models):\n13. DN25-class2-Q3=10\n14. DN25-class2-Q3=16\n15. DN40-class2-Q3=25\n16. DN40-class2-Q3=40\n17. DN50-class2-Q3=40\n18. DN50-class2-Q3=63\n19. DN65-class2-Q3=63\n20. DN65-class2-Q3=100\n21. DN80-class2-Q3=100\n22. DN80-class2-Q3=160\n23. DN100-class2-Q3=160\n24. DN100-class2-Q3=250\n25. DN125-class2-Q3=250\n26. DN125-class2-Q3=400\n27. DN150-class2-Q3=400\n28. DN150-class2-Q3=630\n29. DN200-class2-Q3=630\n30. DN250-class2-Q3=1000\n31. DN300-class2-Q3=1600\n\nNow write all the model_level arrays. Each entry:\n\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": 0.4}, ...]}\n```\n\nDo I need \"value\" to be the structured object with unit_symbol per value? Looking at the R137 example in the prompt: `\"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]` — the values in model_level don't each carry unit (unit is at attribute level) and no footnote_markers. OK so for model_level, simple {model, value} entries are acceptable per the example. I'll follow that. For footnote_markers on specific values — none needed here.\n\nLet me now carefully build each array.\n\n**minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h):**\n```\nDN65-class1-Q3=100: 0.4\nDN80-class1-Q3=100: 0.625\nDN80-class1-Q3=160: 0.64\nDN100-class1-Q3=160: 1.0\nDN100-class1-Q3=250: 1.0\nDN125-class1-Q3=250: 1.56\nDN125-class1-Q3=400: 1.6\nDN150-class1-Q3=400: 2.5\nDN150-class1-Q3=630: 2.52\nDN200-class1-Q3=630: 3.94\nDN250-class1-Q3=1000: 6.25\nDN300-class1-Q3=1600: 10\nDN25-class2-Q3=10: 0.025\nDN25-class2-Q3=16: 0.04\nDN40-class2-Q3=25: 0.0625\nDN40-class2-Q3=40: 0.1\nDN50-class2-Q3=40: 0.1\nDN50-class2-Q3=63: 0.1575\nDN65-class2-Q3=63: 0.1575\nDN65-class2-Q3=100: 0.25\nDN80-class2-Q3=100: 0.25\nDN80-class2-Q3=160: 0.4\nDN100-class2-Q3=160: 0.4\nDN100-class2-Q3=250: 0.625\nDN125-class2-Q3=250: 0.625\nDN125-class2-Q3=400: 1.0\nDN150-class2-Q3=400: 1.0\nDN150-class2-Q3=630: 1.575\nDN200-class2-Q3=630: 1.575\nDN250-class2-Q3=1000: 2.5\nDN300-class2-Q3=1600: 4.0\n```\n\n**transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h):**\n```\nDN65-class1-Q3=100: 0.64\nDN80-class1-Q3=100: 1.0\nDN80-class1-Q3=160: 1.02\nDN100-class1-Q3=160: 1.6\nDN100-class1-Q3=250: 1.6\nDN125-class1-Q3=250: 2.5\nDN125-class1-Q3=400: 2.56\nDN150-class1-Q3=400: 4.0\nDN150-class1-Q3=630: 4.03\nDN200-class1-Q3=630: 6.3\nDN250-class1-Q3=1000: 10\nDN300-class1-Q3=1600: 16\nDN25-class2-Q3=10: 0.04\nDN25-class2-Q3=16: 0.064\nDN40-class2-Q3=25: 0.1\nDN40-class2-Q3=40: 0.16\nDN50-class2-Q3=40: 0.16\nDN50-class2-Q3=63: 0.252\nDN65-class2-Q3=63: 0.25\nDN65-class2-Q3=100: 0.4\nDN80-class2-Q3=100: 0.4\nDN80-class2-Q3=160: 0.64\nDN100-class2-Q3=160: 0.64\nDN100-class2-Q3=250: 1.0\nDN125-class2-Q3=250: 1.0\nDN125-class2-Q3=400: 1.6\nDN150-class2-Q3=400: 1.6\nDN150-class2-Q3=630: 2.52\nDN200-class2-Q3=630: 2.52\nDN250-class2-Q3=1000: 4.0\nDN300-class2-Q3=1600: 6.4\n```\n\n**permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h):**\n100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 400, 630, 630, 1000, 1600, 10, 16, 25, 40, 40, 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 400, 630, 630, 1000, 1600\n\n**overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h):**\n125, 125, 200, 200, 312.5, 312.5, 500, 500, 787.5, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 12.5, 20, 31.3, 50, 50, 78.75, 78.75, 125, 125, 200, 200, 312.5, 312.5, 500, 500, 787.5, 787.5, 1250, 2000\n\n**ratio_q3_q1:** (dimensionless)\n250, 160, 250, 160, 250, 160, 250, 160, 250, 160, 160, 160, then 400 ×19.\n\n**verification_scale_interval (m³):**\nClass1: 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.01\nClass2: 0.00001, 0.00001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.01\n\n**maximum_admissible_pressure (bar):**\nClass1: 16,16,16,16,16,16,16,16,16,16,10,10\nClass2: 16×17 then 10,10 (DN200 gets 16; DN250 & DN300 get 10).\n\n**accuracy_class:**\n1 ×12, 2 ×19.\n\n**nominal_diameter (mm):**\n65,80,80,100,100,125,125,150,150,200,250,300, 25,25,40,40,50,50,65,65,80,80,100,100,125,125,150,150,200,250,300\n\nThat's 9 model_level attributes. Fine.\n\nShould I include all? Maybe skip nominal_diameter in model_level since it's in the model_id and model attributes. But for completeness, keep it. Hmm, it adds 31 more entries. I'll keep it — it's a legitimate varying attribute. Actually, I'll skip it to reduce bloat since it's encoded in the model id and model attributes... No — the point of model_level is a machine-readable attribute×model matrix. I'll include it. OK include.\n\nNow matrix_tables — 5 tables. Columns: [\"meter_size\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"nominal_diameter_mm\", \"verification_scale_interval_m3\"]. Common attributes per table (accuracy_class, temp, pressure...) — but pressure varies within pages 4 and 7. I'll put all varying columns in rows and keep truly-constant ones in \"description\". For page 3 & 5 tables, max pressure is constant (16) — I could still include it as a row column for uniformity. Let me include columns: meter_size, q1_m3_h... hmm naming. Let me use: \"meter_size\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"nominal_diameter_mm\", \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\", \"verification_scale_interval_m3\", \"accuracy_class\". column_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"mm\", \"bar\", \"m³\", null].\n\nRows per table as enumerated above.\n\nTable descriptions:\n- Table 1: \"Metrological characteristics, accuracy class 1 (page 3 of certificate)\". Also common: temperature 0.1/50°C, indicating range 99,999,999 m³, orientation all positions, environmental class C, battery 3.6 V.\n- etc.\n\nNow, certificate.scheme — \"B\".\n\nLet me also double check \"Scheme\": under OIML-CS (2014+) there are Scheme A and B; older certificates (2006-2012) were under the \"OIML Certificate System\" which became Scheme B. So \"B\" is right.\n\nDescription for certified_type: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device. Model WATERFLUX 3070, accuracy classes 1 and 2.\"\n\nNow about the trailing \"101\" artifacts — ignore.\n\nSignature: C. Oosterman, Head Certification Board.\n\nphone: \"+31 78 6332332\". Fax: +31 78 6332309 — not in schema; omit.\n\nNow writing the final JSON. Structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\",\n    \"scheme\": \"B\",\n    \"project_number\": \"12200373\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2012-11-30\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"C. Oosterman\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Head Certification Board\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 78 6332332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"KROHNE Altometer\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Kerkeplaat 12\", \"3313 LC Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"No. 555 Minshen Road\", \"Songjiang Industrial Zone\", \"Shanghai 201612\", \"China\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2006,\n    \"amendment\": null,\n    \"scheme\": \"B\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...]\n}\n```\n\nWait — certificate number: should I include \"revision 1\"? The number as printed: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03 revision 1\". I'll record number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\" — revision info in revision_history. Good.\n\ntype_level characteristics — structured values:\n\n```json\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"min_admissible_temperature\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThe schema shows type_level as `<label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}`. The strict structured value rule says unit_symbol and unit_id. In example under R137 they used unit_symbol in model_level. For type_level, shape: {value, unit, footnote_markers}. I'll use \"unit_symbol\" consistently and include \"unit_id\": null. Let me use keys: value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers.\n\ntype_level entries:\n- admissible_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- indicating_range: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"C\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Battery\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- software_versions: {value: [\"4.0.4_\", \"4.0.10_\", \"4.0.11_\", \"4.0.12_\", \"4.2.2_\", \"4.2.4_\"], unit_symbol: null, ...}\n\nShould footnote_markers be included if empty? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit footnote_markers where empty for cleanliness. Or include empty arrays. I'll omit.\n\nAlso measurement principle could be a type-level characteristic: \"electromagnetic\". I'll put it in description only. Actually, add \"measurement_principle\": {\"value\": \"electromagnetic\"} — informative. The description covers it; adding is fine. I'll add it since it's a stated characteristic: \"based on an electromagnetic principle\". And \"flow_directions\": \"forward and reverse\"? Maybe too much. Keep description. I'll add measurement_principle only... hmm, risk of \"fabrication\" is low since it's stated. I'll include measurement_principle.\n\nAccuracy class overall: since it varies per model, it's in model_level. recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nNow the components — decide: include one entry:\n\n```json\n\"components\": [\n  {\n    \"role\": \"electronic calculating/indicating device\",\n    \"type_designations\": [],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {}\n  }\n]\n```\n\nHmm, \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\" — it does name one, without a type designation. I'll include it. Fine.\n\nTest reports:\n```json\n\"test_reports\": [\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01\", \"pages\": 41, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n]\n```\nNote the 4th id is printed \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\" — likely OCR/typo for R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01. Should I preserve exactly? \"Don't fabricate\" — but normalizing an obvious typo... I'll keep as printed: \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\"? Hmm. It's clearly the same-format ID with a typo (period instead of slash). I'll transcribe as printed to be faithful: \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\". Hmm, actually extraction should be faithful to the doc; I'll keep the printed form. But it may look like an error... I'll keep printed form.\n\nAlso maybe include annexes info... not in schema, skip.\n\nRevision history:\n```json\n[\n  {\"revision\": \"Initial\", \"date\": \"2012-11-29\", \"changes\": null},\n  {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2012-11-30\", \"changes\": \"Adjustment in page numbering.\"}\n]\n```\nInitial changes is \"-\" → null.\n\nNow assemble model_family.models with attributes: I'll include {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 65, \"q3_m3_h\": 100}? Redundant. Keep attributes minimal: {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 65}. Q3 is in the id. OK.\n\nActually, to keep JSON manageable, maybe omit per-model attributes since model_level covers everything. The schema says models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. attributes is presumably optional per-variant attrs. I'll include label and leave attributes out. Hmm, schema shape shows attributes{} — I'll include a small attributes dict with accuracy_class and nominal_diameter for each. That's 31 × small dict — fine.\n\nLet me write labels: \"DN65, class 1, Q3=100 m³/h\" etc. That duplicates id info. Maybe labels: \"DN65\" hmm. I'll give label like \"DN65 electromagnetic water meter, accuracy class 1, Q3 = 100 m³/h\". Too verbose ×31. Simpler: label: \"DN65, accuracy class 1, Q3=100\". OK.\n\nNow, let me write the whole thing carefully. I must ensure valid JSON — no trailing commas, proper unicode (m³ and °C fine in JSON).\n\nLet me build model_level arrays in fu